this is in some ways an homage I think  that you have certainly omage is here  too John Ford and some of the Great's  you know you're standing in the shadows  of some of those great directors who  have made these yeah it's always tough  when you make a film in a genre like the  Western or even the gangster genre which  some of the best films ever made reside  so immediately you're going to be  compared to those films but I didn't the  film doesn't flinch from America's dark  and unforgiveable past of attempted  genocide towards our indigenous people  but it also is I think speaking to some  of the modern concerns that we have this  very vast racial and cultural divide  that seems to be getting wider by the  day so I wanted to pay homage to to Ford  some of the psychological westerns of  Anthony Mann but also make it feel very  very contemporary now Christian you  worked with Scott before out of the  furnace and this is your second teaming  with him is it where what was it about  this character Blocher he's a very  interesting complex guy in this movie  what was it about that that appealed to  you when you saw this grip well one of  the things that really appealed was the  pacing of looking at you know how people  would have behaved at that time the you  know the fact you don't go racing your  horse you're walking it and then there's  sudden the violence is sudden and  extreme when it does happen and then  goes again of this of this solitary  nature of a man who's been out in this  for behringer for years and years and  years and what I liked watching Ken  Burns's Civil War documentary thinking  that that was you know how blocker would  have grown up and I kind of sort of  imagined even though they were meant to  be older many you know 12 13 14 year  olds of fighting in the Civil War and so  I pictured him kind of being in the  hornet's nest at Shiloh and that's kind  of really been his entire life there's  been firing and he's a dutiful soldier  he does exactly what he is he's meant to  do I know that with Scott he tells his  stories beautifully  is and this is a absolutes whole  compliment kind of an invisible director  like he doesn't want to put his own sort  of character stamp it onto the film he  just wants to kind of let it be seen and  I love that and really really love  working with him he gets extraordinary  performances from actors because he lets  them you know be comfortable and come at  it with their strengths and and for me I  found it really fascinating in terms of  the learned hatred that the blocker had  to you know a choir and a crew to do  what he did the personal tragedies of  his brothers in arms who he had seen  murdered and often butchered brutally as  compared to the larger atrocity of the  of the genocide and and knowing that  although Blocher has had to create this  fierce hatred of yellow hawk he knows  full well he's a smart man he knows full  well that if he was in yellow hawk shoes  he would behave exactly the same way and  I see it as very much an indictment of  politicians that you know a blocker is  is is doing what he has been instructed  to do and does it extraordinarily well  and then suddenly the whims of DC change  and his his attitude is no longer the  correct one and suddenly you know he's  threatened with the court-martialing and  everything and seeing these men who  really have to live day to day life and  death and witnessing that compared to  the safety of the politicians in  Washington who then he struck these men  and threatened them and just how  disgusting that that attitude is and  that correlates obviously very much to  what was going on today and and and  since since 1892 and then long before as  well this role I've seen you in so many  movies and so many great roles dances  with what on and on and on in your  career well how does this one fit into  that to this particular store  and what it's saying for today's  audiences what did you respond to well I  think it just went a little bit further  than perhaps the ones that I've done  before you know and that's what always  is what invites me to do a part is or  it's a challenge to play someone who's  actually dying you know over a long  period of time what does it feel like to  know that you only have a certain number  of days to live and it's not anything  that I could recall from my own life to  bring to the screen at that time nothing  ever other than having watched maybe  another person slowly die but that's the  only reference I had to the whole thing  and well where I went with it was to you  know concentrate on the lack or loss of  hope of a future what does one do at the  end of one's life but yeah that that's  that was the other part of the whole  experience was that aspect of it Cory  Anka you have worked with Christian  before in the new world right well yes  all three of you this is a big reunion  here yeah yes and this role meant a lot  to you too you've worked in a lot up  with a lot outside you're an activist in  indigenous issues and things and so can  you talk about it from that point of  view for you what it means to be in this  movie and what you do in your own life  as well when I was a little girl it was  actually always a dream of mine to do a  Western there was something so romantic  and so beautiful about it and I always  longed to live back in the day when you  traveled like a like nomads on horseback  and were one with nature and that going  back to the the the simplicity and the  basics of life and so when I first read  the script I  just say like yay this is one that I can  you know check off my bucket list of of  a film that I would love to be a part of  and you know from I guess the activist  standpoint of you you know I really feel  like Hollywood is moving in the  direction of being fine finally becoming  more inclusive and I think it's so  important and I really really respected  and appreciated Scott and Christians  advocacy for really really making sure  